1
00:00:00,043 --> 00:00:01,044
GridBot Builder.

2
00:00:01,634 --> 00:00:02,784
GridBot Blueprint.

3
00:00:03,340 --> 00:00:04,074
Lesson one.

4
00:00:04,626 --> 00:00:05,355
Introduction.

5
00:00:06,470 --> 00:00:07,616
Education only.

6
00:00:08,175 --> 00:00:09,489
Not financial advice.

7
00:00:10,009 --> 00:00:12,061
This is general information and

8
00:00:12,061 --> 00:00:14,264
doesn't consider your objectives,

9
00:00:14,264 --> 00:00:16,164
financial situation or needs.

10
00:00:16,684 --> 00:00:18,448
Crypto is high risk; you could

11
00:00:18,448 --> 00:00:20,139
lose all the money you put in.

12
00:00:20,659 --> 00:00:22,724
Past performance is not a reliable

13
00:00:22,724 --> 00:00:24,434
indicator of future results.

14
00:00:25,484 --> 00:00:27,540
The prices are public historical

15
00:00:27,540 --> 00:00:29,302
daily closes, marked Example.

16
00:00:29,822 --> 00:00:31,889
A close is the last price of the day.

17
00:00:33,044 --> 00:00:34,943
No order is sent from this lesson.

18
00:00:35,942 --> 00:00:37,918
By the end of this series you will

19
00:00:37,918 --> 00:00:39,968
understand what a grid bot, a small

20
00:00:39,968 --> 00:00:42,017
program that places these orders

21
00:00:42,017 --> 00:00:43,920
for you, does, when it can capture

22
00:00:43,920 --> 00:00:45,531
bounces, and where it loses.

23
00:00:46,051 --> 00:00:48,061
You will know how to plan one, test

24
00:00:48,061 --> 00:00:49,961
it on paper, run it, check it, and

25
00:00:49,961 --> 00:00:52,008
change the capital you give to bots

26
00:00:52,008 --> 00:00:53,870
only under written risk rules.

27
00:00:54,390 --> 00:00:55,787
This is education, not

28
00:00:55,787 --> 00:00:57,106
a promise of results.

29
00:00:57,626 --> 00:00:59,237
Price often moves sideways,

30
00:00:59,237 --> 00:01:00,987
bouncing between the same top

31
00:01:00,987 --> 00:01:02,738
and bottom instead of leaving.

32
00:01:03,258 --> 00:01:04,967
A grid is a ladder of waiting

33
00:01:04,967 --> 00:01:06,677
orders, called limit orders,

34
00:01:06,677 --> 00:01:08,164
built for that bouncing.

35
00:01:08,684 --> 00:01:10,528
When price runs one way and stays

36
00:01:10,528 --> 00:01:12,706
there, the grid has nothing left to do.

37
00:01:13,087 --> 00:01:14,763
And if price falls through the

38
00:01:14,763 --> 00:01:16,272
bottom, you're left holding

39
00:01:16,272 --> 00:01:17,612
coin that is worth less.

40
00:01:18,282 --> 00:01:19,623
By the end you will know

41
00:01:19,623 --> 00:01:20,908
what a grid bot places.

42
00:01:22,037 --> 00:01:24,116
You will see that idea on

43
00:01:24,116 --> 00:01:26,091
one real path from 2023.

44
00:01:27,291 --> 00:01:28,799
You will be able to say the

45
00:01:28,799 --> 00:01:30,252
stop rule in one sentence.

46
00:01:31,205 --> 00:01:33,051
When a chart says Example, it

47
00:01:33,051 --> 00:01:34,897
means a real past price used

48
00:01:34,897 --> 00:01:36,743
as an example, not a forecast.

49
00:01:37,793 --> 00:01:39,035
Crypto is a digital

50
00:01:39,035 --> 00:01:40,432
asset, such as bitcoin.

51
00:01:40,952 --> 00:01:42,628
You do not need to have bought

52
00:01:42,628 --> 00:01:43,913
any before this lesson.

53
00:01:44,291 --> 00:01:46,259
An exchange is the website or app

54
00:01:46,259 --> 00:01:48,591
where you swap Australian dollars for

55
00:01:48,591 --> 00:01:50,631
crypto, and crypto back to dollars.

56
00:01:51,151 --> 00:01:52,827
This course does not name one,

57
00:01:52,827 --> 00:01:54,503
and it does not send an order.

58
00:01:55,248 --> 00:01:56,813
Cash is money you can spend.

59
00:01:57,215 --> 00:01:58,837
Here that means dollars, or a

60
00:01:58,837 --> 00:02:00,600
dollar-pegged token, treated

61
00:02:00,600 --> 00:02:02,011
like cash in this lesson.

62
00:02:02,531 --> 00:02:04,723
Coin is the crypto, such as bitcoin.

63
00:02:05,773 --> 00:02:08,119
An order is an instruction to buy or sell.

64
00:02:08,571 --> 00:02:10,378
A buy order swaps cash for coin.

65
00:02:10,899 --> 00:02:13,262
A sell order swaps coin back to cash, and

66
00:02:13,262 --> 00:02:15,626
you can only sell coin you already hold.

67
00:02:16,676 --> 00:02:18,446
A bot is software that places

68
00:02:18,446 --> 00:02:20,068
and refills orders for you.

69
00:02:20,588 --> 00:02:22,432
You start with some cash and some

70
00:02:22,432 --> 00:02:24,108
coin, so there is something to

71
00:02:24,108 --> 00:02:25,839
buy with and something to sell.

72
00:02:25,888 --> 00:02:28,253
A grid bot places waiting buy orders

73
00:02:28,253 --> 00:02:30,381
on lines below the price and sell

74
00:02:30,381 --> 00:02:32,510
orders on lines above it, inside a

75
00:02:32,510 --> 00:02:34,244
top and a bottom you choose.

76
00:02:34,764 --> 00:02:36,440
The space between your top and

77
00:02:36,440 --> 00:02:38,172
your bottom is called the band.

78
00:02:38,281 --> 00:02:40,386
The sells need some coin, so a grid

79
00:02:40,386 --> 00:02:42,336
starts with part cash and part

80
00:02:42,336 --> 00:02:44,285
coin, and that is a description,

81
00:02:44,285 --> 00:02:46,157
not advice about what to hold.

82
00:02:47,207 --> 00:02:48,913
A limit order is an order that

83
00:02:48,913 --> 00:02:50,477
waits at a price you choose.

84
00:02:50,996 --> 00:02:52,561
It does not chase the price.

85
00:02:52,980 --> 00:02:54,544
Chasing the price would mean

86
00:02:54,544 --> 00:02:55,997
moving the order to follow

87
00:02:55,997 --> 00:02:57,561
wherever the price has gone.

88
00:02:58,410 --> 00:03:00,485
An order fills when the price reaches

89
00:03:00,485 --> 00:03:02,217
its line and the trade happens.

90
00:03:02,679 --> 00:03:04,885
Until then, the order is only waiting.

91
00:03:05,935 --> 00:03:07,891
Think of a ladder leaned on a wall.

92
00:03:08,032 --> 00:03:10,378
The rungs wait, and they do not chase you.

93
00:03:10,654 --> 00:03:12,945
A rung is one price where an order waits.

94
00:03:13,352 --> 00:03:15,530
The gap is the space between two rungs.

95
00:03:15,819 --> 00:03:17,864
The ladder is the analogy, and the

96
00:03:17,864 --> 00:03:20,136
chart beside it is the real past path.

97
00:03:21,186 --> 00:03:22,596
The range is the top rung

98
00:03:22,596 --> 00:03:23,725
and the bottom rung.

99
00:03:24,245 --> 00:03:26,368
The levels are the rungs between them.

100
00:03:26,718 --> 00:03:29,008
The stop is the written rule for leaving.

101
00:03:29,857 --> 00:03:31,701
The range is the top line and the

102
00:03:31,701 --> 00:03:33,545
bottom line, taken from the path.

103
00:03:34,023 --> 00:03:37,248
The chart is bitcoin's real daily

104
00:03:37,248 --> 00:03:40,817
closes from 19 July to 16 October 2023.

105
00:03:41,337 --> 00:03:42,790
Every close in this window

106
00:03:42,790 --> 00:03:44,522
stayed between those two lines.

107
00:03:45,308 --> 00:03:47,713
Six gaps of about 900 dollars each sit

108
00:03:47,713 --> 00:03:50,275
between the bottom line and the top line.

109
00:03:50,794 --> 00:03:53,021
Each gap is about 900 dollars:

110
00:03:53,021 --> 00:03:55,248
the top line minus the bottom

111
00:03:55,248 --> 00:03:56,733
line, divided by six.

112
00:03:57,253 --> 00:03:58,588
It is one step between

113
00:03:58,588 --> 00:04:00,071
lines, not the whole band.

114
00:04:00,591 --> 00:04:02,430
Seven lines make those six gaps.

115
00:04:02,950 --> 00:04:04,738
The levels are the prices on the

116
00:04:04,738 --> 00:04:06,414
rungs where limit orders wait.

117
00:04:07,400 --> 00:04:09,691
Buy orders wait on lines below the price.

118
00:04:10,127 --> 00:04:12,082
Sell orders wait on lines above it.

119
00:04:12,563 --> 00:04:15,063
Each fill changes what you hold: a buy

120
00:04:15,063 --> 00:04:17,729
fill adds coin, and a sell fill adds cash.

121
00:04:18,249 --> 00:04:19,366
You choose the band,

122
00:04:19,366 --> 00:04:20,651
the gaps and the money.

123
00:04:20,935 --> 00:04:22,387
The bot places each order.

124
00:04:23,394 --> 00:04:25,187
After a fill, the bot places the

125
00:04:25,187 --> 00:04:26,908
opposite order one rung away.

126
00:04:27,428 --> 00:04:29,606
A buy fill gets a sell one rung higher.

127
00:04:29,993 --> 00:04:32,222
A sell fill gets a buy one rung lower.

128
00:04:32,742 --> 00:04:34,363
You still write the stop, and

129
00:04:34,363 --> 00:04:36,039
you act when that rule is met.

130
00:04:36,270 --> 00:04:38,505
The bot does not decide the stop for you

131
00:04:38,505 --> 00:04:40,628
unless you turned that setting on, and

132
00:04:40,628 --> 00:04:42,695
you write which one before you start.

133
00:04:43,429 --> 00:04:44,770
Think of a market stall.

134
00:04:45,202 --> 00:04:47,316
The stallholder buys stock a little

135
00:04:47,316 --> 00:04:49,430
cheaper and sells it a little dearer,

136
00:04:49,430 --> 00:04:51,050
and pays a fee on every sale.

137
00:04:51,500 --> 00:04:53,228
A grid can do that while price

138
00:04:53,228 --> 00:04:54,668
bounces inside the band.

139
00:04:55,188 --> 00:04:57,709
A bounce means price moves down and back

140
00:04:57,709 --> 00:05:00,078
up, or up and back down, and stays inside.

141
00:05:00,598 --> 00:05:02,834
One buy at a lower line and the later

142
00:05:02,834 --> 00:05:04,917
sell of that same coin is one pair.

143
00:05:05,437 --> 00:05:07,589
On these daily closes, the July to

144
00:05:07,589 --> 00:05:09,821
October example window finished

145
00:05:09,821 --> 00:05:11,893
only three pairs one rung apart.

146
00:05:12,413 --> 00:05:14,145
The sell line is higher, so the

147
00:05:14,145 --> 00:05:15,989
cash that comes back is more than

148
00:05:15,989 --> 00:05:17,944
the cash that left, before the fee.

149
00:05:18,237 --> 00:05:19,690
A fee is what the exchange

150
00:05:19,690 --> 00:05:21,254
charges when an order fills.

151
00:05:21,435 --> 00:05:23,055
The fee comes out of the gap.

152
00:05:23,282 --> 00:05:25,014
If the fees are larger than the

153
00:05:25,014 --> 00:05:26,578
gap, the pair keeps nothing.

154
00:05:26,759 --> 00:05:28,002
This is not a promise.

155
00:05:28,522 --> 00:05:30,326
The pair finishes only if price

156
00:05:30,326 --> 00:05:32,199
does bounce and both orders fill.

157
00:05:33,249 --> 00:05:34,478
The daily close is the

158
00:05:34,478 --> 00:05:35,707
last price of the day.

159
00:05:36,128 --> 00:05:37,928
Crypto trades all day, so the

160
00:05:37,928 --> 00:05:39,336
day is cut at a set time.

161
00:05:39,856 --> 00:05:42,061
The daily close is the end of

162
00:05:42,061 --> 00:05:44,554
each UTC day, 11am Sydney time in

163
00:05:44,554 --> 00:05:47,142
daylight saving, 10am otherwise.

164
00:05:47,662 --> 00:05:49,646
This is a simple candle that shows

165
00:05:49,646 --> 00:05:51,322
the day's high, low and close.

166
00:05:51,795 --> 00:05:53,725
Traders call it a candle because

167
00:05:53,725 --> 00:05:55,654
the full version has a thick body

168
00:05:55,654 --> 00:05:57,386
and a thin wick, like a candle.

169
00:05:57,818 --> 00:05:59,326
Here we only need the high,

170
00:05:59,326 --> 00:06:00,555
the low and the close.

171
00:06:00,889 --> 00:06:04,068
This candle is 19 July 2023.

172
00:06:04,588 --> 00:06:05,817
The high, the low, and

173
00:06:05,817 --> 00:06:07,102
the close are labelled.

174
00:06:07,995 --> 00:06:10,124
The example is Bitcoin's public

175
00:06:10,124 --> 00:06:11,780
daily price in US dollars.

176
00:06:12,300 --> 00:06:15,499
From 19 July 2023 to 16 October

177
00:06:15,499 --> 00:06:18,442
2023, every one of those days

178
00:06:18,442 --> 00:06:20,873
closed inside the band.

179
00:06:21,393 --> 00:06:23,066
The close crossed the seven

180
00:06:23,066 --> 00:06:24,812
lines 28 times in that window.

181
00:06:25,332 --> 00:06:27,644
The count is an example, not a forecast.

182
00:06:28,164 --> 00:06:29,952
We do not need to read each day.

183
00:06:30,218 --> 00:06:31,560
The shape is the lesson,

184
00:06:31,560 --> 00:06:32,974
and it is marked Example.

185
00:06:33,494 --> 00:06:36,250
Worked check: 19 July

186
00:06:36,250 --> 00:06:39,330
2023 to 16 October 2023.

187
00:06:39,850 --> 00:06:41,359
The low is the bottom line.

188
00:06:41,839 --> 00:06:43,305
The high is the top line.

189
00:06:43,825 --> 00:06:45,669
Nothing on this card is an order.

190
00:06:46,632 --> 00:06:49,566
Those 28 crossings are not 28 results.

191
00:06:50,086 --> 00:06:52,288
A crossing only means the close moved

192
00:06:52,288 --> 00:06:54,355
from one side of a line to the other.

193
00:06:54,797 --> 00:06:56,508
A pair is finished only when a

194
00:06:56,508 --> 00:06:58,362
buy and its later sell have both

195
00:06:58,362 --> 00:07:00,094
filled, and only after the fee.

196
00:07:00,594 --> 00:07:02,493
The price can cross many lines and

197
00:07:02,493 --> 00:07:04,392
still leave you holding coin if it

198
00:07:04,392 --> 00:07:06,012
walks out through the bottom.

199
00:07:06,549 --> 00:07:08,057
You can buy part of a coin.

200
00:07:08,606 --> 00:07:11,566
Here we buy one hundredth

201
00:07:11,607 --> 00:07:12,668
of a bitcoin, 0.01.

202
00:07:12,668 --> 00:07:15,632
In this example a buy waiting at the

203
00:07:15,632 --> 00:07:18,921
lower of these two lines was reached on

204
00:07:18,921 --> 00:07:21,902
17 August 2023, and the sell one line

205
00:07:21,902 --> 00:07:24,883
above was reached on 29 August 2023.

206
00:07:25,403 --> 00:07:27,191
The card shows the gap, then the

207
00:07:27,191 --> 00:07:28,755
two fees, then what is left.

208
00:07:29,064 --> 00:07:30,888
That is about 8 dollars 46

209
00:07:30,888 --> 00:07:32,974
after fees, for one small pair.

210
00:07:33,494 --> 00:07:35,393
It happens only if the price comes

211
00:07:35,393 --> 00:07:37,069
back up through the sell line.

212
00:07:37,173 --> 00:07:38,905
If the price leaves through the

213
00:07:38,905 --> 00:07:40,693
bottom, you still hold the coin,

214
00:07:40,693 --> 00:07:42,257
and the pair never finishes.

215
00:07:42,449 --> 00:07:44,237
This card is not money you made.

216
00:07:44,635 --> 00:07:46,981
Past results don't predict future results.

217
00:07:53,969 --> 00:07:55,757
Now watch what you hold when the

218
00:07:55,757 --> 00:07:57,768
price leaves the band and stays out.

219
00:07:58,032 --> 00:07:59,652
Above the band the sells have

220
00:07:59,652 --> 00:08:01,439
filled and you are holding cash,

221
00:08:01,439 --> 00:08:02,892
with nothing left to sell.

222
00:08:03,296 --> 00:08:04,860
Below the band the buys have

223
00:08:04,860 --> 00:08:06,313
filled and you are holding

224
00:08:06,313 --> 00:08:08,045
coin, with nothing left to buy.

225
00:08:08,327 --> 00:08:09,719
That is why the way down

226
00:08:09,719 --> 00:08:11,478
matters most: every buy fills,

227
00:08:11,478 --> 00:08:13,236
and you are left holding coin.

228
00:08:15,286 --> 00:08:17,521
Here is that stop rule on the real path.

229
00:08:17,942 --> 00:08:20,302
It triggers if a daily close is outside

230
00:08:20,302 --> 00:08:22,589
the band, through the top or the bottom,

231
00:08:22,589 --> 00:08:25,098
and the next daily close is still outside.

232
00:08:25,618 --> 00:08:27,070
Then the idea is finished.

233
00:08:27,450 --> 00:08:29,544
You stop the bot, which cancels the

234
00:08:29,544 --> 00:08:31,414
waiting orders, and do what you

235
00:08:31,414 --> 00:08:33,507
wrote: keep the coin or swap to cash.

236
00:08:34,027 --> 00:08:36,009
A bot's automatic stop is a price

237
00:08:36,009 --> 00:08:37,697
that can fire during the day.

238
00:08:38,217 --> 00:08:39,917
This course uses the two daily

239
00:08:39,917 --> 00:08:41,752
closes, unless your written plan

240
00:08:41,752 --> 00:08:43,540
says you turned on a price stop.

241
00:08:43,972 --> 00:08:45,312
This is a teaching rule,

242
00:08:45,312 --> 00:08:46,490
not a recommendation.

243
00:08:47,010 --> 00:08:48,630
The stop is your written rule

244
00:08:48,630 --> 00:08:50,083
for when you stop the bot.

245
00:08:50,316 --> 00:08:52,449
On 23 October 2023 the close

246
00:08:52,449 --> 00:08:54,861
finished above the top line, and

247
00:08:54,861 --> 00:08:57,181
the next day was still above it.

248
00:09:02,231 --> 00:09:03,683
Through the top, the sells

249
00:09:03,683 --> 00:09:05,359
have filled and you hold cash.

250
00:09:05,468 --> 00:09:07,312
Through the bottom, the buys have

251
00:09:07,312 --> 00:09:09,155
filled and you hold coin that you

252
00:09:09,155 --> 00:09:11,166
bought above where the price is now.

253
00:09:11,525 --> 00:09:12,978
That loss can keep growing

254
00:09:12,978 --> 00:09:14,095
until the stop acts.

255
00:09:14,400 --> 00:09:16,244
You stop the bot either way, then

256
00:09:16,244 --> 00:09:17,808
you follow the written rule.

257
00:09:17,917 --> 00:09:19,705
The bot does not decide the stop

258
00:09:19,705 --> 00:09:21,604
unless you turned that setting on.

259
00:09:21,877 --> 00:09:23,298
EXAMPLE, made up: three

260
00:09:23,298 --> 00:09:24,878
buys fill on the way down.

261
00:09:25,398 --> 00:09:27,975
One hundredth of a bitcoin at the

262
00:09:27,975 --> 00:09:30,266
middle line is 276 dollars 30.

263
00:09:31,120 --> 00:09:36,654
One hundredth at 26,730 is 267 dollars 30.

264
00:09:37,506 --> 00:09:42,688
One hundredth at 25,830 is 258 dollars 30.

265
00:09:43,208 --> 00:09:45,848
Together that is 801 dollars 90.

266
00:09:46,368 --> 00:09:48,543
A little below the band, at a

267
00:09:48,543 --> 00:09:50,917
made-up price of 24,000, that

268
00:09:50,917 --> 00:09:52,994
coin is worth 720 dollars.

269
00:09:53,514 --> 00:09:55,140
You are down 81 dollars 90

270
00:09:55,140 --> 00:09:57,076
before fees, and the loss keeps

271
00:09:57,076 --> 00:09:58,935
growing until your stop acts.

272
00:09:59,985 --> 00:10:01,560
A common mistake is thinking

273
00:10:01,560 --> 00:10:03,068
the bot predicts the price.

274
00:10:03,857 --> 00:10:04,607
It does not.

275
00:10:05,173 --> 00:10:05,955
It only waits.

276
00:10:06,325 --> 00:10:07,833
Another mistake is starting

277
00:10:07,833 --> 00:10:09,006
